    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d270-a3">

                <pb facs="0489" n="429" sameAs="frus:frus1868p2/pg429"/>

                <head>[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">City of Mexico,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1868-02-21"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 21, 1868.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your two communications of the 5th and 9th
                    of January reached me only on the 17th instant.</p>
                <pb facs="0490" n="430" xml:id="pg430"/>
                <p>As the rebellion in Yucatan has now happily been suppressed by the commendable
                    vigor of this government, it is unnecessary for me to enter into the questions
                    relating thereto, raised in your letters.</p>
                <p>It is very clear the interests of our commerce with this country will be
                    promoted, if this government shall be able, as I trust will be the case
                    hereafter, promptly to crush all attempts that may be made to subvert the
                    constitutional order.</p>
                <p>I am, very respectfully, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>E. L. PLUMB.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. Juanes y Patrullo,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">United States Consul, Merida.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
